WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.590
Good evening, everybody, and
welcome to the second concert

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.290
of our 2017-2018
season here in MIT.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.080
So glad to see you
all this evening.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.120
I notice an extra
layer of clothing

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.208
on everybody, especially me.

00:00:21.208 --> 00:00:23.500
I almost wore my coat for
the performance here tonight.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.780
That's very, very cold
out there tonight.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.920
Jean Sibelius and
Johannes Brahms--

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.950
though both of them wrote in
many, many different forms,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.020
they are, without question, two
of our greatest symphonists.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:43.360
They lived in a time where they
were going against the stream.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.520
Sibelius in the 1920s--

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.370
lots of atonal music
is around and about.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.100
The Rite of Spring is
10 years old by the time

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.680
this symphony was
written and performed.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.360
But Sibelius's music sounds as
if it's been around forever.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.170
Brahms, of course, is still well
under the shadow of Beethoven,

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.420
as he liked to say,
over and over again,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.190
writing very classically
with very great attention

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:12.250
to form and with a
classical-sized orchestra

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.390
for the most part, in a time
where Wagner and Mahler is

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.840
about to write music that
expands the orchestra

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.770
to a great degree.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:29.080
But here, for mostly, that's
where the similarities end.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.720
Both of them dealt with
personal issues and in a way--

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.530
doesn't show up in their
music at least on the surface.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.450
Brahms, by his growing up
poor and his initial work

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.100
in a bordello-- that was his
very first job as a pianist--

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.700
suffered great abuse
emotionally and spiritually,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.670
without getting into great
detail, from this experience.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.040
And it would be something that
would haunt his relationships,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.040
especially his
relationships with women

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.510
for the rest of his life.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.350
Jean Sibelius, very much
in the spirit of Mozart,

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.840
had trouble with money, had
trouble dealing with money,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.180
had trouble keeping
money, and also

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.250
dealt severely with alcoholism.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.040
And this was something that
would affect his marriage

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.230
to greater or lesser degrees for
the entirety of his marriage.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.350
The symphonies, on the surface,
both look very classical.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.860
They're four-movement entities,
and in Brahms's the symphony

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.460
especially, you can see very
clear-cut allegro movement

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.160
with an intro, a
slow movement, what

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.340
seems like it's going to be
a scherzo movement, and then

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.690
a large finale very much
in the line of Beethoven.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.860
Sibelius the same-- it
looks allegro on the page.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:45.700
We have an allegro.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.450
We have something that's
not quite a slow movement

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.840
and then a scherzo and
then a large-scale finale.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.340
But they both
depart from the norm

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.170
in very, very interesting ways.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.680
Brahms is pushing the
envelope harmonically

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.788
and also rhythmically.

00:03:00.788 --> 00:03:02.830
For those that are going
to be hearing this piece

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.413
for the first time tonight, how
many of you, by show of hands,

00:03:05.413 --> 00:03:08.330
will be hearing Brahms 1
for the first time tonight?

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.680
Awesome, awesome.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.770
Remember, somebody is always
hearing it for the first time.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:16.880
This is a great reminder
for orchestras, too, right?

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.760
You're going to hear a lot
of syncopation in this piece,

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.790
now, not in a Duke
Ellington kind of way,

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.220
but Brahms is going to
make you think something

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.810
is 1 when it's actually 2.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.360
Or it might actually be
the middle of the bar.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.340
He's always throwing you
off just a little bit.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.590
And in the orchestra,
the layering of rhythm,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.410
the 3 against 2
against 4 going on,

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.990
is prevalent throughout
much of this piece,

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.350
and it was very daring for
its time, as conservative

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:47.360
as it seems to us now in
the post-jazz era, right?

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.760
Harmonically, it lives.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.470
It descends from
Beethoven for sure.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.170
It has hints of Wagner
about it, but it

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:58.670
doesn't push us to any
kind of new world, right?

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.610
The Sibelius is very
striking in the sense

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:05.120
that when you hear the piece,
especially its opening,

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.840
it sounds like this is
music that has been around

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:12.170
since before Gregorian
chant, and that

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.560
is because instead of working
with the normal major-minor

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.430
idea, our typical Western
tonality, major-minor,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.470
he's working modally.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.142
In fact, one might
even go so far

00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:25.100
as to say the main melody
of the whole symphony

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:30.020
is (SINGING) D, E, F,
G, A, the first five

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.880
notes of a minor scale.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.760
And to that, for
expressive purposes,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.430
he tacks on (SINGING) B
natural, C at the end of it.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.610
And if you think about
that in your brain,

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.040
and you know where
that sits on the piano,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.610
it's all the white keys if
you go from D to D, right?

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:47.540
So it's modal.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:48.890
It's the Dorian scale.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.550
And what gives this
piece it's archaic sound

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:56.630
is this reliance on the
modal, and in some ways,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.310
this is not a new
thing to its time.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.890
Certainly, Vaughan
Williams and Holst

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.010
are experimenting with
modality over in the UK.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.800
But for continental Europe, this
would have been very striking

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.740
in 1923 when we think
about Stravinsky,

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.228
Schoenberg, Hindemith, Bartok.

00:05:11.228 --> 00:05:13.520
All of these people are on
the scene, and for something

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.090
to sound, at least on the
surface, so very simple,

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:21.830
so very motet-like, this
piece, was really striking.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.530
And so they share that,
being progressive while

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.870
seeming very conservative
in their time.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.700
And I think the
farther and farther

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:32.600
along we get into the future,
the more both of these pieces

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.380
and both of these composers
seem even more daring,

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.090
year after year after year.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.660
As is typically
our form, I'm not

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.050
going to talk again between
the pieces, so you're welcome.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:51.360
But my hope for you is,
especially for the Sibelius--

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.880
which we are on the
Sixth Symphony now.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.830
The MIT Symphony has
been participating

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.710
with the Brookline Symphony
in a chronological cycle

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.630
of the Sibelius
seven symphonies,

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.400
and so we're almost there.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.510
And I think what
you're going to hear,

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.160
especially in this orchestra, is
that they play Sibelius really

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.830
well, and it sits well in them.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:15.060
And the beauty and
voluptuousness of this music,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.553
in a way, bespeaks
the difficulty of it.

00:06:17.553 --> 00:06:20.220
You're going to see them working
very hard up here on this piece

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.510
but playing it very, very well.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.093
The same with the Brahms-- it's
a notoriously difficult piece.

00:06:24.093 --> 00:06:26.550
He's a notoriously difficult
composer to perform.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.630
But the structure of it
is very easy to ascertain,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.570
and there's no question
that this is still

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:37.980
music that moves us, that
hits us to some degree,

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.110
no matter how ancient
it may be, right?

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.050
The Brahms is almost
50 years younger.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:44.550
Excuse me.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.920
It's almost 50 years
older than the Sibelius,

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.400
so keep that in mind as you
listen to both of these pieces

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:50.770
tonight.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:51.780
We hope you enjoy them.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.840
I kindly ask out of respect
for the performers that

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.880
put an hour after
hour after hour

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.300
to present these pieces to
you that you please turn off

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.670
all the electronics tonight.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:03.720
Plus, they're not period.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.780
They weren't around
in 1876 nor 1923,

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.220
so we can be part of a
performance practice tonight.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.600
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:14.070
Have a happy Thanksgiving,
and safe travel if you do.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.140
Enjoy

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.140
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:33.140
